<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- NowCastTab — the body of the NowCast window: watch boxes + storm-based warnings, and today's storm
     reports. One of three mode bodies (see Controls/Windows/TemporalWindow, registered once per mode); the
     window supplies the surface, the title and the pin, so this is content only.
     ⚠️ The file keeps its "Tab" name from when the three were tabs of one Timeframe window. It is the same
     control, unchanged by the split; renaming all three would be churn. Bound to the coordinator MapViewModel.

       ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
       │  ›  Radar                                            │  ← collapsed
       │  ⌄  Warnings                                         │  ← OPEN by default — the one
       │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
       │  │ 7 active warnings                        │        │     section you actually watch
       │  │ Updated 12:04 PM · checking every 15s    │        │
       │  └──────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
       │  [ ] ■ Tornado                            0          │
       │  [x] ■ Severe thunderstorm                7          │
       │  Opacity ──────●───────────                          │
       │  ›  Watches                                          │  same control as Warnings,
       │  ›  Storm reports                                    │  a different view model
       └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     The body holds the "now"-oriented live-conditions options: the SPC Watch boxes and the storm-based
     Warning polygons (Tornado / Severe Thunderstorm). Both are active, event-driven current-conditions
     alerts, so they live under NowCast rather than ForeCast, in the UI and in the VM layer alike (their own
     WatchesViewModel / WarningsViewModel).

     EVERY SECTION IS AN EXPANDER (Styles.xaml SectionExpanderStyle) — header = the section title,
     body = its controls. Warnings is the only one open on load; the rest start closed, so the window
     opens short and you open what you came for. Expanded state is view-only and NOT persisted: reopening
     the window gives you these defaults again.
     ⚠️ The window sizes to its content ONCE, at open (WindowManager's sizeToContent), so expanding a
     section afterwards grows the body past the window. That is known and accepted for now — a scroller
     is coming, hand-rolled rather than WinUI's.

     ⚠️ WARNINGS SITS ABOVE WATCHES, which puts it out of the map's Z-order (watches are drawn under
     warnings). Deliberate: it is the section you actually watch during severe weather, so it gets the
     slot under Radar rather than the one its layer order would give it.

     ⚠️ WATCHES AND WARNINGS ARE SEPARATE SECTIONS, not one "Watches and warnings". Each carries its
     OWN card describing its OWN active set, and one header cannot introduce two cards. They are also
     visually identical — the same two type rows, the same two colours — so the titles are the only
     thing telling you which is which.

     Both alert sections are the SAME control (Composites/PhenomOverlayInput) bound to a different view
     model, exactly as watches.js and warnings.js are the same JS factory with a different config. Storm
     reports stay their own control — the PastCast window shows that same block, so it is content of TWO
     hosts.

     ⚠️ This tab does NOT arm or clear NowCast; that is the NowCast key in the bottom bar's centre cluster.
     A tab body configures a mode, the key runs it. -->
<UserControl x:Class="Anvil.Controls.Composites.NowCastTab"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:comp="using:Anvil.Controls.Composites"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <Grid VerticalAlignment="Top" RowSpacing="8">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- radar -->
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- warnings -->
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- watches -->
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- storm reports (today) -->
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- The radar layer everything else is drawn over; the value behind it is global, which is only
             safe because Past and Now are mutually exclusive so those two windows can't both be open. -->
        <Expander Grid.Row="0" Header="Radar" Style="{StaticResource SectionExpanderStyle}">
            <comp:RadarOverlayInput ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel.Radar, Mode=OneWay}"/>
        </Expander>

        <!-- The imminent-threat polygons — open on load, and above Watches. -->
        <Expander Grid.Row="1" Header="Warnings" IsExpanded="True"
                  Style="{StaticResource SectionExpanderStyle}">
            <comp:PhenomOverlayInput AlertNoun="warnings"
                                     ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel.Warnings, Mode=OneWay}"/>
        </Expander>

        <!-- The large county-aggregated watch areas. Same control as Warnings above. -->
        <Expander Grid.Row="2" Header="Watches" Style="{StaticResource SectionExpanderStyle}">
            <comp:PhenomOverlayInput AlertNoun="watches"
                                     ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel.Watches, Mode=OneWay}"/>
        </Expander>

        <!-- ⚠️ The title is HERE, not inside StormReportsInput: that control is shared by BOTH temporal
             windows, and a window owns its own section structure while the control is just content. -->
        <Expander Grid.Row="3" Header="Storm reports" Style="{StaticResource SectionExpanderStyle}">
            <comp:StormReportsInput ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel.StormReports, Mode=OneWay}"/>
        </Expander>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
